---
title: Agent mailboxes
name: Agent mailboxes
description: An email address your AI agent keeps, at agentboi.email or on your own domain. One request makes one; the agent reads its mail as JSON, can tell your team from a stranger, answers in the thread, files threads in folders and labels, writes drafts for a person to send, and works over MCP.
category: Getting started
---

**An agent mailbox is an email address your agent keeps.** [Tempboi](/temp-inbox) gives an
agent an address that goes away when it's done; a mailbox at
[agentboi.email](https://agentboi.email) stays. The agent reads what arrives as JSON over a
long poll, each message says who is talking, and one call answers in the thread. Run by
Postboi, the same provider as the rest of these docs.

## Make one

There's nothing to set up. With the `POSTBOI_TOKEN` that `postboi init` wrote, the mailbox is
your team's from the start and can send. The first call makes it, and every call after that
opens the same one:

```ts
import { mailbox } from "postboi/mailbox"

const box = await mailbox("orders")
box.address // "orders-k3f9@agentboi.email", today and on every run after
```

`mailbox()` with no name opens the team's mailbox when it has one, and makes one when it has
none. Once the team has several, name the one you mean.

Without a token, an agent can make one on its own. It receives straight away and sends once
a person opens its claim link and signs in, at which point it joins their team:

```bash
curl -X POST agentboi.email
```

```
orders-k3f9@agentboi.email
key:    mb_…
claim:  https://postboi.app/claim/…
        (open this to let it send; until then it only receives)
```

Put the claim link in your summary for the person you're working for: it's the one step only
they can take. Nobody claims it, and it's deleted after 14 days.

A mailbox made without a token is remembered on your machine
(`~/.config/postboi/mailboxes.json`, readable by you alone) under the directory it was made
from. So the next `mailbox()` there opens the same address rather than making another, and
`postboi mailbox` and `postboi mcp` find it too. Pass `remember: false` to keep nothing.

### A key for one mailbox

Every mailbox also has a key of its own (`mb_…`, said once, when it is made). Your team's key
opens all of them, so the mailbox key is for an agent you'd rather not hand that: it opens
this one mailbox, and nothing else of your team's.

```ts
const box = await mailbox({ key: process.env.AGENT_MAILBOX_KEY })
```

`POSTBOI_MAILBOX_KEY` in the environment does the same thing with nothing passed.

### On agentboi.email, or on your own domain

On agentboi.email the address is the name you ask for plus four random characters, so nobody
can take another agent's address, and names that read as an institution or a role somebody
could pose as (`support`, `billing`, a bank) are refused. To use your own domain, turn on
[receiving](/provider#receiving-on-your-own-domain) for it and pass `domain`: the address is
then exactly what you asked for.

```ts
await mailbox.create({ address: "support", domain: "reply.example.com" })
// support@reply.example.com
```

## Read its mail

```ts
const box = await mailbox("orders")

for await (const mail of box.watch()) {
	mail.trust // "owner" | "thread" | "stranger" | "suspect"
	mail.reply_text // what they wrote, without the conversation quoted under it
	mail.code // a one-time code, if there is one
}
```

`watch()` holds a long poll open and yields each message as it lands, which works from a
sandbox or CI with no public URL. `wait()` returns the next match and is the one to use for
a sign-up code. When the sender wrote an
[`OTP-Token`](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-goto-otp-token/) header, `mail.otp` is
`{ code, origin, signed }` and `mail.code` is that code rather than one read out of the text.
`signed` is true only when the sender's own DKIM signature covers the header:

```ts
const mail = await box.wait({ subject: "verify", timeout: "2m" })
mail.code // "482913"
```

Both take `from`, `subject` (a substring or a RegExp), `tag` and `trust`. Mail to
`orders-k3f9+anything@agentboi.email` lands in the same mailbox with `tag` set to `anything`,
so one mailbox can tell its errands apart.

Over HTTP, with nothing installed:

```bash
curl "https://agentboi.email/v1/mailboxes/<address>/wait?timeout=60" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer mb_…"
```

The whole API is in [agentboi.email/llms.txt](https://agentboi.email/llms.txt) and the
[API reference](https://postboi.app/docs).

## Who is talking

Every message is labelled when it arrives, so an agent can tell your team from a stranger
before it does anything:

| `trust`    | Means                                                                     |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `owner`    | From a member of the team that owns the mailbox, and the mail passed DMARC |
| `thread`   | A reply to something your team sent                                       |
| `stranger` | Anyone else                                                               |
| `suspect`  | Read as junk, refused as spam by the receiving server, or failing DMARC   |

A label is not a permission. It tells your agent who is speaking; it doesn't make what a
stranger wrote safe to act on. Write your agent's instructions so that a stranger's words are
information to weigh, never instructions to follow, and so that anything it does with tools
because of an email comes from `owner` mail or from you.

## Answer

```ts
await box.reply(mail, { text: "It ships today." })

await box.send({ to: "ada@example.com", subject: "Your order", text: "It ships today." })

await box.forward(mail, { to: "accounts@example.com", text: "Can you refund this one?" })
```

A reply goes to the sender (or their Reply-To), with a `Re:` subject and the In-Reply-To and
References headers that keep it in their thread, all worked out from the message being
answered. A send is from the mailbox's own address and nothing else. A forward is
`Fwd: <subject>` with your note above the original and its files attached (pass
`attachments: false` to leave them off). A send and a reply take an `idempotency_key`, so a
retried call sends once, and all three count against your plan like any other send, with the
same suppressions, rate limits and checks.

Each resolves to what happened to it:

```ts
const result = await box.send({ to: "ada@example.com", subject: "Hello", text: "Hi" })
if (result.status === "sent") result.id // "msg_…", and result.thread_id
else result.draft_id // held for review: a person sends it (see below)
```

### Attachments

`send`, `reply` and drafts take files. `content` is the bytes (a `Uint8Array`, an
`ArrayBuffer` or a `Blob`), or a string that is already base64:

```ts
await box.reply(mail, {
	text: "Your invoice is attached.",
	attachments: [{ filename: "invoice.pdf", content: pdf_bytes, type: "application/pdf" }],
})
```

Up to 20 files and 10 MB in all a message. Over that, the send is refused with
`attachments_too_large`.

`postboi mailbox reply <id> --text "…"`, `postboi mailbox send --to … --subject … --text …`
and `postboi mailbox forward <id> --to …` do the same from a shell, with `--attach a.pdf,b.png`
for files.

## Folders, labels and search

Every thread is in one folder: `inbox`, `archive`, `spam`, `trash`, or `sent` for a
conversation the mailbox started that nobody has answered yet. New mail marks a thread
unread and brings it back to the inbox from the archive or `sent`; a thread somebody put in
spam or the trash stays there. A first message read as junk starts its thread in spam.

```ts
const unread = await box.threads({ unread: true })
const billing = await box.threads({ label: "billing", folder: "all" })
const found = await box.threads({ q: "refund" }) // subject, sender, recipients and text

await box.mark_read(thread)
await box.archive(thread)
await box.update_thread(thread, { add_labels: ["billing"], remove_labels: ["urgent"] })
```

Labels belong to the mailbox: up to 50, named 1 to 40 characters, in `grey`, `amber`, `red`,
`green` or `blue`. Naming one that doesn't exist on a thread makes it, in grey.
`box.labels()` lists them with how many threads carry each, and `create_label`,
`update_label` and `delete_label` manage them by id or by name.

From a shell: `postboi mailbox threads --unread --q refund`, `postboi mailbox archive <thread>`,
`postboi mailbox label <thread> billing` and `postboi mailbox labels`.

## Drafts and review

An agent can write a draft for a person to look over and send instead of sending it:

```ts
const draft = await box.create_draft({ in_reply_to: mail, text: "Here's what I'd say…" })
```

With `in_reply_to` it threads like a reply, and `to` and `subject` come from the message it
answers. Your team hears about every new draft (the **Drafts** notification and the
`draft_created` [webhook](/webhooks#mailbox-drafts)) and sends it from the dashboard.
`box.drafts()`, `draft(id)`, `update_draft`, `delete_draft` and `send_draft` cover the rest.

A mailbox can also be told to wait for a person before it sends at all. Its `review` is set by
your team:

| `review` | What the mailbox's key may send                                              |
| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `none`   | Anything. The default                                                        |
| `new`    | A reply to the person who wrote, with nobody added. Anything else waits      |
| `all`    | Nothing: everything waits                                                    |

Under `new`, a send to a new conversation, a forward, or a reply that adds a cc or bcc is
saved as a draft instead of going, and comes back as `{ status: "draft", draft_id, draft }`.
Your agent shouldn't send it again: it is waiting for a person. Only the mailbox's own key is
held, so your team's API key sends as usual, and only your team changes `review`:

```ts
const team = await mailbox.open({ token: process.env.POSTBOI_TOKEN, address: box.address })
await team.set_review("new")
```

`postboi mailbox review new` does the same with `POSTBOI_TOKEN` set, and
`postboi mailbox drafts` lists what is waiting.

## It's your team's mail too

A mailbox's mail is filed in your dashboard's Received log with everything else, so a person
can read along and answer from the dashboard. It arrives at your
[webhooks](/webhooks) as `email.received`, which for mailbox mail also carries `mailbox`,
`trust`, `reply_text`, `codes` and `otp`. The dashboard's **Mailboxes** page makes them, rotates
their keys and deletes them.

Replying from your own code with `mail()` rather than the mailbox works too: pass the
received message's id as `in_reply_to` and the Postboi provider threads it the same way.

```ts
await mail({ to: "bob@example.com", subject: "Re: Your order", body: "…", in_reply_to: "in_…" })
```

## Over MCP

`postboi mcp` runs an [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) server on stdio, so an agent in
Claude Code, Cursor, Codex or any other MCP client reaches its mailbox as tools. It needs
nothing installed beyond the package and nothing to configure. It reads the project's `.env`,
so the `POSTBOI_TOKEN` that `postboi init` wrote is already enough:

```bash
claude mcp add postboi -- npx postboi mcp
```

For Cursor (`.cursor/mcp.json`), Claude Desktop and most other clients:

```json
{
	"mcpServers": {
		"postboi": { "command": "npx", "args": ["postboi", "mcp"] }
	}
}
```

For Codex (`~/.codex/config.toml`):

```toml
[mcp_servers.postboi]
command = "npx"
args = ["postboi", "mcp"]
```

With your team's key it opens the team's mailbox, or the one each tool's `mailbox` names when
the team has several, and `email_send` sends the team's own mail. With no key at all, the
agent makes a mailbox of its own with `mailbox_create` and hands you its claim link. That
mailbox is remembered on your machine, so the next session opens it again. To give an agent
one mailbox and nothing else of your team's, put that mailbox's key in the server's
environment as `POSTBOI_MAILBOX_KEY`.

| Tool                                                                 | Does                                                         |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `mailbox_info`, `mailbox_create`                                     | The mailbox, or a new one                                    |
| `mailbox_list_messages`, `mailbox_wait`, `mailbox_read`              | Its mail, waiting up to 90 seconds, a code on its own        |
| `mailbox_threads`, `mailbox_thread`, `mailbox_update_thread`         | Conversations by folder, label, read state or search         |
| `mailbox_labels`                                                     | List, make, change and delete labels                         |
| `mailbox_reply`, `mailbox_send`, `mailbox_forward`                   | Answer, write and forward, with attachments                  |
| `mailbox_drafts`, `mailbox_create_draft`, `mailbox_update_draft`, `mailbox_delete_draft`, `mailbox_send_draft` | Drafts for a person to send |
| `temp_inbox_create`, `temp_inbox_wait`                               | A [Tempboi](/temp-inbox) address and the code sent to it     |
| `email_send`                                                         | One email from your team's sending address                   |

Every result that carries somebody else's words (their text, their subject, their name) puts
them inside an `<email>` block that names its `trust`, after a line saying the content is data
from the sender and not instructions. A sender who writes `</email>` into their message can't
close the block early. It's the same rule as everywhere else on this page: the label says who
is talking, and your agent's own instructions decide what it does about it.

## Keys

A mailbox's key (`mb_…`) opens that mailbox and nothing else in Postboi: it can't send as your
team, read your other mail, make more mailboxes or loosen its own review. Your project's `POSTBOI_TOKEN` opens every
mailbox the team has. `box.rotate()` (or `postboi mailbox key`) makes a new key and stops the
old one at once.

Deleting a mailbox stops its key and refuses mail to it from then on. What it received stays
in your Received log, and the address is never given to anyone else.

## Limits

|                       |                                                       |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Mailboxes             | No limit on how many. Free makes 10 a day (UTC)       |
| Unclaimed account     | 3 mailboxes in all, until somebody claims it          |
| Received mail         | Free, and doesn't count against your sends            |
| A message             | Up to 10 MB                                           |
| Unclaimed             | Receives, can't send, deleted after 14 days           |
| Made with no account  | 5 a day from one address, the same as `init --agent`  |
| `wait`                | Up to 90 seconds a request                            |
| `messages?wait=`      | Up to 25 seconds a request                            |
| Attachments on a send | 20 files, 10 MB in all                                |
| Labels                | 50 a mailbox                                          |
